module TrinityFpgaDdr3ReaderT27;

// DDR3 read path of the AX7203 (issue #62): a Wishbone burst reader on UberDDR3's
// user port with consumer (A), wired by fpga/ax7203/ddr3/tms_ddr3_reader.v (the
// 128-bit split into u64 ports) into fpga/ax7203/ddr3/tms_ddr3_ax7203.v
// when that top is read with `define DDR3_READER (make ... DDR3_APP=reader). x16
// only: one Wishbone word is 128 bits, 16 bytes, byte n at bits [8n, 8n+7].
// Like fpga_ddr3_pattern.t27 it owns the report line: the status lines of
// fpga_ddr3_status.t27 pass through its arbiter to the line emitter.
//
// After o_calib_complete it runs `runs` runs (0 = until reset); run r writes and
// reads format r & 1 (0 baseline2, 1 dense5) with the key of pair r >> 1, so runs 2p
// and 2p+1 carry the same `trits` logical trits in the two layouts. A run
//   1. fills the region: writes every word 0 .. W-1 (W = ceil(trits / 64) or
//      ceil(trits / 80)) with the TMEM device-code bytes of the trit stream
//      (no UART: the board generates the data), until every write is acknowledged;
//   2. reads every word back through the burst reader, and consumer (A) decodes
//      each acknowledged word, compares its lanes with the regenerated stream and
//      accumulates the counts, the dot product and the checksum;
//   3. reports the run on the UART.
// tools/ddr3_read_model.py states the stream, the layouts and every result in Python.
//
// Trit stream (tools/ddr3_read_model.py): trit i is lane i & 15 of block i >> 4, the
// 16 lane codes of raw(K, b) = (K xor post(lin(mix(b)))) mod 2^32 with a pair 11 read
// as 00, K = lin(lin((seed << 32) | pair) + KADD), lin the nine xorshift steps of the
// pattern test, mix and post one nonlinear step each (without them the +1 and -1 counts
// and the dot product of a region of aligned ranges would not depend on the key; they do
// not change that the baseline2 checksum of such a region is 0 for every key, nor make
// the counts and dot product random-like: docs/hardware.md, "DDR3 read path (#62)");
// lanes after `trits` are logical zeros. A baseline2 word holds blocks
// 4w .. 4w+3 (64 lanes, lane j at bits [2j, 2j+1], four per byte, earliest lowest),
// a dense5 word blocks 5w .. 5w+4 (80 lanes, byte n = the dense5 code of lanes 5n ..
// 5n+4, the code of docs/format.md and t27/rtl/bram_trit_codec.t27). The generator is a pipeline of
// four stages (G1 raw blocks, G2 padding mask, G3 encoded word, cur) that advances
// by one word per write taken (fill) or per read acknowledged (read): cur holds the
// word being written or the lanes the next acknowledged word must decode to.
//
// Burst reader (Wishbone, UberDDR3 79d8fd3e: docs/hardware.md, "What the #61
// pattern test needs"): a request is taken on a clock with stb and not stall; a
// presented request is never withdrawn or changed. A new request is presented only
// while the requests taken and not yet acknowledged, counting the one being taken,
// stay below `cap`, so at most `cap` are outstanding. Acks cannot be held off: consumer
// (A) takes one word in every clock, with no FIFO and no ready, so an ack is always
// accepted in its clock (consumer_stalls counts acks that arrive while cons_ready is
// low; cons_ready is constant true, so it is 0 by construction and measures nothing). The
// cap therefore only bounds the reader; it is reported, as are the clocks it held a request
// back. A phase ends at its W-th ack, so the words of a run are W by construction; the
// acks themselves are counted apart from that: every ack while the reader is in neither
// FILL nor READ (stray acks), reported with every run, so an extra or a displaced ack shows
// as stray acks > 0 (a lost ack stops the phase: t line). Every ack after calibration (all
// acks) is reported too, but all acks = the runs' fill and read words + stray acks holds by
// construction (each phase takes exactly its W acks), so it adds nothing to stray = 0 and
// only guards the report path.
//
// Consumer (A), one 128-bit word per controller clock, nine register stages:
// S1 the acknowledged word with its expected lanes; D the decoders on four 32-bit chunks
// (20 dense5 lanes or 16 baseline2 lanes each, invalid groups in bits 55:48, the layout of
// the codec's decode) and the checksum word; S2 the chunks assembled into 80 or 64 lanes and
// the invalid groups added; S3 the lane compare and
// per-class popcounts of the +1 and -1 lanes (class a = lane mod 8, activation a + 1);
// S4 per-class sums; S5 pair sums and the weighted pairs; S6 the word's +1, -1 and
// weighted sums; S7 the accumulators and the word's dot product; S8 the dot accumulator.
//
// Counters of a read (docs/hardware.md, "DDR3 read path (#62)"):
//   cycles        controller clocks from the first read request presented to the last ack, inclusive
//   words         acks of the phase, which ends at the W-th: W by construction
//   bus bytes     16 per word consumed (consumer (A)'s valid chain; the same acks)
//   payload bytes bytes of each word that hold at least one logical trit (ceil(trits/4), ceil(trits/5))
//   padding bytes 16 - payload bytes, per word (so padding = bus - payload is an identity)
//   scale/metadata bytes  0: this region stores no scale and no metadata
//   logical trits lanes consumed below `trits` (padding lanes excluded)
//   command stalls clocks with a request presented and o_wb_stall high
//   wait stalls   clocks with at least one request outstanding and no ack
//   consumer stalls acks while consumer (A) is not ready (0 by construction)
//   max outstanding, cap holds (extra): the most requests outstanding at a clock, and the
//                 clocks with no request presented while requests remained (the cap held them)
//   stray acks, all acks (extra, since calibration, not cleared per run): acks while the
//                 reader is in neither FILL nor READ (the evidence), and every ack (an identity
//                 with the words and stray acks)
//
// Report lines (tag, a 32 bits, b 40 bits), after the status reporter's H and S lines:
//   q a=trits per run   b=(READER_FORMAT << 32) | (byte lanes << 24) | cap
//                       (READER_FORMAT 2: runs end with the x line; 1, a6d9745f: with h)
//   k a=seed            b=controller clocks since reset when calib_complete was seen (low 40)
//   v a=runs (0 = until reset)   b=watchdog clocks
// then per run:
//   a a=run   b=(format << 32) | (lanes per word << 24)
//   f a=fill words acknowledged   b=fill cycles (first write request presented to last write ack)
//   g a=fill max outstanding      b=fill command stalls
//   c a=words                     b=cycles
//   y a=consumer stalls           b=bus bytes
//   p a=scale/metadata bytes      b=payload bytes
//   d a=bad words                 b=padding bytes
//   n a=invalid groups            b=logical trits
//   o a=max outstanding           b=command stalls
//   w a=cap holds                 b=wait stalls
//   u a=-1 lanes (low 32 bits)    b=+1 lanes (low 40 bits)
//   s a=dot product bits 63:40 (64-bit two's complement)   b=dot product bits 39:0
//   h a=checksum bits 63:32       b=checksum bits 31:0
//   x a=stray acks since calibration   b=all acks since calibration (low 40 bits)
// and at the end
//   t a=acks of the open phase   b=(read phase << 36) | (format << 32) | run   (watchdog; stops)
//   z a=runs done   b=bad words over all runs                                 (after `runs` runs)
// As in fpga_ddr3_pattern.t27, the module-level assignments are not lowered by the
// pinned gen-c: the functions are checked in C against tools/ddr3_read_model.py and
// the module in Icarus against a behavioural Wishbone memory (tests/test_ddr3_reader.py).
